  "figure_captions": {
    "01_ecosystem_composition": "Figure 1. Exact ecosystem counts at association, package, package-version and advisory grains. Each panel has its own denominator. The population is the processed vulnerable-association export, not all published packages or versions.",
    "02_quality_profiles": "Figure 2. Exact percentages of processed records with missing/invalid severity, a recorded zero score, or a blank description, within each ecosystem. Zero is retained separately because its source meaning is not fully documented. These percentages describe record completeness rather than missingness mechanisms.",
    "03_severity_bands": "Figure 3. Severity-band percentages of processed associations within each ecosystem. Bands use numeric scores: zero, (0,4), [4,7), [7,9), and [9,10]. Unknown includes absent or invalid values. Scores do not establish exploitability or safety.",
    "04_weighting_ecdf": "Figure 4. Empirical cumulative distributions of numeric severity for associations and for ecosystem-advisory IDs using each ID's maximum observed score. Each line excludes unknown scores and states a different estimand. Advisory aliases remain unresolved and conflicting scores can affect the maximum.",
    "05_package_version_burden": "Figure 5. Distribution of distinct observed affected versions per ecosystem-qualified package. Boxes show Q1, median and Q3; whiskers use 1.5 IQR and dots show outside values. The logarithmic axis reveals skew in a vulnerable-only population without release dates.",
    "06_advisory_reach": "Figure 6. Distinct ecosystem-qualified package versions linked to each ecosystem-advisory ID. Boxes show Q1, median and Q3; whiskers use 1.5 IQR and dots show outside values. Counts measure observed advisory reach rather than independent security events.",
    "07_exposure_concentration": "Figure 7. Lorenz curves for observed affected-version counts across ecosystem-qualified packages, sorted by burden. Axes express shares of packages and observed affected versions within each ecosystem. A package-version associated with multiple advisories contributes once to that package's burden.",
    "08_package_count_relationship": "Figure 8. Logarithmic hexbin views of distinct affected-version count versus distinct advisory-ID count per package. Hexagon color shows package frequency. Spearman correlation summarizes rank association only; no regression, causal effect, or temporal trend is fitted.",
    "09_severity_histogram": "Figure 9. Exact association-weighted numeric severity distribution from processed data. Recorded zeros are counted separately from positive scores, which use 0.5-point bins on [0,10], left-closed and right-open except the final bin. Missing or invalid scores are excluded from numeric bins and reported separately; zeros do not establish safety."
  },
  "elapsed_seconds": 17.836899299989454,
  "authorship_status": "Complete evidence-based interpretations prepared with AI assistance. This computation record does not certify individual student contributions or rehearsal.",
  "figure_interpretations": {
    "01_ecosystem_composition": "PyPI contributes 448,966 of 884,137 associations (50.78%), while npm contributes 15,052 of 17,583 observed packages (85.61%). The reversal shows why repeated package-version/advisory links and distinct package counts answer different parts of the Phase 1 distribution question. These are counts within the vulnerable-association export, so neither share estimates prevalence among all ecosystem packages.",
    "02_quality_profiles": "No processed association has missing/invalid numeric severity or a blank description, but recorded-zero rates differ: RubyGems 30.84%, PyPI 30.33%, Cargo 23.62% and npm 17.15%. Thus syntactically complete fields still require semantic review before cross-ecosystem comparison. Zero is retained as a recorded value; its exporter meaning is undocumented and these observations cannot establish a random missingness mechanism or safety.",
    "03_severity_bands": "Scores of at least 7 account for 165,965/375,126 npm associations (44.24%) and 146,459/448,966 PyPI associations (32.62%). Because this export has no unknown numeric scores, the known-score and full-association denominators coincide in these percentages. The profiles answer the observed impact-distribution question, but repeated affected versions and source scoring conventions prevent interpreting the difference as ecosystem-wide risk.",
    "04_weighting_ecdf": "The npm high/critical share changes from 44.24% among 375,126 scored associations to 13.65% among 16,847 ecosystem-advisory maxima; PyPI changes only from 32.62% to 30.24%. Repeated links to many affected versions can substantially change an impact summary, so choosing the counting unit matters more than simply choosing a chart. Each observed ecosystem-advisory pair has one numeric score here, but unresolved aliases mean advisory IDs still cannot be assumed to represent independent vulnerabilities.",
    "05_package_version_burden": "PyPI's 1,148 observed packages have median 24 affected versions and IQR 50, compared with npm's median 1 and IQR 0 across 15,052 packages. npm nevertheless reaches 4909 versions for one package, making the long tail visible on a log axis; its zero IQR means counts above one meet the mechanical 1.5-IQR rule, not that they are invalid. Resistant summaries preserve this structure without deleting legitimate extremes, and no complete-release denominator or chronology is available.",
    "06_advisory_reach": "Median affected-package-version reach is 36 for 6,815 PyPI ecosystem-advisory IDs, 2 for RubyGems and 1 for npm and Cargo. npm's maximum of 4909 versus PyPI's 1420 shows that a small median can coexist with very broad observed advisories. This partly answers Phase 1's version-distribution question and supports inspection of high-burden IDs, but counts represent observed associations rather than installations, exploitation or alias-resolved security events.",
    "07_exposure_concentration": "The top 151 of 15,052 npm packages account for 52.41% of its 195,515 observed affected versions; the corresponding PyPI group of 12 accounts for 15.87%. This concentration suggests that a descriptive review focused on high-burden observed packages can cover many affected-version records. Each top group is ceil(1% of observed packages), and the curve measures neither installed dependency reach nor the probability a package is vulnerable.",
    "08_package_count_relationship": "Spearman correlations between distinct affected-version count and advisory-ID count are pypi 0.386 (n=1,148), npm 0.467 (n=15,052), rubygems 0.585 (n=1,233), cargo 0.241 (n=150). A defined positive coefficient indicates rank association; a constant count makes the coefficient undefined. Hexbin aggregation avoids overplotting and exposes ties and sparse tails; without a fitted trend there are no model residuals to diagnose, and no causal or temporal conclusion follows.",
    "09_severity_histogram": "Recorded zero forms a separate mass of 218,849/884,137 associations (24.75%); missing/invalid scores number 0 and never enter the numeric bins. Among positive recorded scores, exact modes include 7.5 (94,685 associations) and 9.8 (66,977), producing pronounced peaks rather than a smooth bell-shaped distribution. Half-point bins reveal broad shape while the exact frequency table preserves discrete spikes; these association-weighted peaks reflect repeated versions and score conventions, not independent vulnerability counts."
  }
